  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is a fosinoprilat-type dianionic phosphinic acid and carboxylic acid with neutral fraction 0, logD -0.6, TPSA 94.9, and no basic site; this calibrated physicochemical prior supports poor passive permeability and F below 20%.",
      "card_ids": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "C193 shows enalaprilat, the closest active-diacid ACE inhibitor analog, has a human jejunal effective permeability of only 0.013 ± 0.009 ×10^-4 cm/s, an extremely low value that supports poor intestinal absorption for this compound class.",
      "card_ids": ["C193"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "C177 and C195 show enalaprilat permeability is not increased by net water absorption in human jejunum or by hypotonic perfusion in rat jejunum, indicating the absorption barrier is intrinsic and not overcome by paracellular enhancement.",
      "card_ids": ["C177", "C195"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "C165 shows DG013A, a phosphinic acid compound, has Caco-2 Papp below 1.0 ×10^-6 cm/s with no measurable efflux, supporting low passive permeability for the phosphinic acid motif and no efflux-mediated rescue.",
      "card_ids": ["C165"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "C205 shows PLGA nanoparticles raise enalaprilat ex vivo Papp to 4.61 ×10^-6 cm/s, but the query has no such formulation; this is a formulation caveat, not evidence of intrinsic high oral bioavailability.",
      "card_ids": ["C205"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "C139 shows SQ-29852 has 60% human absorption despite low Caco-2 permeability, but that molecule contains a basic amino group and is a different phosphonate ACE inhibitor; it does not transfer to the query, which has no basic center.",
      "card_ids": ["C139"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C193", "C177", "C195", "C165", "C205"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental oral bioavailability or matched oral/IV exposure data for fosinoprilat itself.",
    "No direct query permeability, efflux, or intestinal metabolism measurement; L5 records are for analog molecules.",
    "No data on possible active uptake transporters that could raise fosinoprilat absorption.",
    "No information on prodrug conversion or clinical formulation effects that could alter F."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains low and is strengthened by L5. The closest active-diacid ACE inhibitor analog enalaprilat shows extremely low human and rat jejunal effective permeability that is not enhanced by luminal conditions, and a phosphinic acid compound shows very low Caco-2 permeability without efflux. These coherent mechanistic data support low F for the dianionic fosinoprilat-type query. C205 is a formulation-dependent counterexample and C139 is non-transferable due to its basic amino group. Confidence stays moderate because direct fosinoprilat bioavailability data remain absent."
}

